# Skin photo accuracy

Full runbook: `backend/docs/SKIN-ACCURACY-LOOP.md`.

A photo becomes a **morphology group** (mụn viêm / mụn ẩn / milia / sần sùi-texture / thâm /
mụn thịt / nếp gấp cổ…). Every routine and tip is derived from it, so a wrong group makes
everything downstream wrong while still reading confidently.

## Single source of truth

- Wording + taxonomy: `internal/service/ai/vision_morphology.go`
- Which group wins: `internal/service/ai/morphology_classify.go` (plain Go, unit-tested)
- Admin, onboarding and check-in all concatenate the shared block. **Never fork it into one
  pipeline** — `TestVisionMorphologyRules_SharedAcrossPipelines` fails if you do.

## When the user pastes eval output

They ran `cmd/eval-morphology-export` + `TestMorphologyEvalLive` against production data.
The output lists accuracy per group and every miss. Diagnose in this order:

1. Model named the wrong group on a clear photo → tighten `vision_morphology.go`.
2. Model described it right but the group came out `unknown` → a missing phrase in the cue
   lists in `morphology_prose.go` (cheap, most common).
3. Both look right and it still misses → only then discuss a stronger
   `DADIARY_OPENAI_VISION_MODEL`.

Then run the offline tests, and have them re-run the last command to confirm the number
moved. Never claim an accuracy improvement without that before/after.

## Constraints to keep

- Only ask the user a follow-up question when `ShouldAskUser` is true, i.e. the look-alikes
  differ on care direction (calm-first vs gentle base). Milia vs closed comedones vs skin
  tags share the same advice, so asking would dent trust for nothing.
- `GroupUnknown` means the cue parser did not recognise the wording — a parser gap, not an
  unreadable photo. Never surface it as uncertainty to a user.
- Exported eval photos are real user faces: keep them gitignored, delete after each round.
- Never put `skin_context` (what the user reported about their own skin) into anything
  served publicly; `StripInternalAnalysisFields` guards the share payload.
